From: Eric Tanner <eric.tanner@harvia.com>
Subject: [PATCH] regulator: tps65219: fix irq_data allocation size (heap overflow at probe)

tps65219_regulator_probe() allocates its per-IRQ data arrays with

    irq_data = devm_kmalloc(tps->dev, pmic->common_irq_size, GFP_KERNEL);

i.e. the NUMBER of IRQs in bytes, not the size of the array. On the
TPS65214 that is a 36-byte allocation that the loop below then fills
with 36 x 24 bytes of {dev, type, rdev} pointers, overwriting ~800 bytes
of neighbouring slab objects on every boot, about 0.52 s into the kernel
(right after the GPIO rails and before the EEPROM, I2C buses and panel).

On the SignaTouch board this showed up as random oopses a few tens of
milliseconds later in whatever happened to own the clobbered objects:

  Unable to handle kernel paging request at virtual address 005f524f534e4573
  pc : __kmalloc_cache_noprof  lr : __request_region  (omap_i2c_probe)
  (the bogus "pointer" is the ASCII of a kthread name "irq/NN-SENSOR_2_WARM"
   handed out twice from a corrupted kmalloc-32 freelist)

  Unable to handle kernel execute from non-executable memory ffff000001fe0c20
  lr : klist_node_init  (at24_probe -> nvmem_register -> device_add)

With no watchdog running that early, each one leaves the unit hung dark.
Allocate the arrays with the proper element size. Upstream fixed the same
bug in 2025 ("regulator: tps65219: Fix devm_kmalloc size allocation").

Signed-off-by: Eric Tanner <eric.tanner@harvia.com>
---
--- a/drivers/regulator/tps65219-regulator.c
+++ b/drivers/regulator/tps65219-regulator.c
@@ -436,7 +436,9 @@
 					     pmic->rdesc[i].name);
 	}
 
-	irq_data = devm_kmalloc(tps->dev, pmic->common_irq_size, GFP_KERNEL);
+	irq_data = devm_kmalloc(tps->dev,
+				pmic->common_irq_size * sizeof(*irq_data),
+				GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!irq_data)
 		return -ENOMEM;
 
@@ -459,7 +461,9 @@
 					     irq_type->irq_name, irq, error);
 	}
 
-	irq_data = devm_kmalloc(tps->dev, pmic->dev_irq_size, GFP_KERNEL);
+	irq_data = devm_kmalloc(tps->dev,
+				pmic->dev_irq_size * sizeof(*irq_data),
+				GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!irq_data)
 		return -ENOMEM;
 
